po}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/28b844aa435d7e3ce6aeee6819042ef3ac907d51/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T23:39:59Z","email":"cdwilhite0601@gmail.com","name":"Craig D Wilhite"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T23:39:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Refine App Studio projects, models, and conversations (#548)\n\n* Refine provider portal interfaces and shared UI components\n\n* Fix App Studio review findings","tree":{"sha":"6150e6ee0105a2975a4e96a7f7b26461eded763a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/6150e6ee0105a2975a4e96a7f7b26461eded763a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/28b844aa435d7e3ce6aeee6819042ef3ac907d51","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/28b844aa435d7e3ce6aeee6819042ef3ac907d51","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKDI4Yjg0NGFhNDM1ZDdlM2NlNmFlZWU2ODE5MDQyZWYzYWM5MDdkNTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/58ed4d8c1c085cfd4e8d8f8b143ff9d1d103a3da","sha":"58ed4d8c1c085cfd4e8d8f8b143ff9d1d103a3da","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/58ed4d8c1c085cfd4e8d8f8b143ff9d1d103a3da"}],"sha":"28b844aa435d7e3ce6aeee6819042ef3ac907d51","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/28b844aa435d7e3ce6aeee6819042ef3ac907d51"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/58ed4d8c1c085cfd4e8d8f8b143ff9d1d103a3da/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T17:27:53Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T17:27:53Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Front APIExport virtual workspaces on the hub, and complete the self-host recipe (#546)\n\n* Let the hub front APIExport virtual workspaces on a single-shard platform\n\nA provider watches every workspace consuming its APIExport through kcp''s\nvirtual workspace, whose address kcp copies from\nShard.spec.virtualWorkspaceURL into\nAPIExportEndpointSlice.status.endpoints[].url. Point that at a service DNS\nname and only consumers inside the shard''s own cluster can dial it — fine\nfor platform providers, fatal for self-hosted ones:\n\n  error starting endpoint watcher: Get \"https://alpha-shard-kcp.kcp-system\n  .svc.cluster.local:6443/services/apiexport/.../clusters/*/api\":\n  no such host\n\nThe failure is quiet and badly localized. Templates live in the provider''s\nown workspace and are reached over the provider kubeconfig, a different\nURL that works — so the provider comes up healthy, serves its API, wins\nits leader election, reconciles Templates, and simply never acts on a\ntenant Instance.\n\nForward /services/apiexport/... to kcp so a single-shard or embedded\nplatform can publish its one public hub address as the virtual-workspace\nURL. An operator then exposes one entrypoint with one certificate instead\nof standing up a second one that exists solely for virtual workspaces.\nEmbedded kcp now defaults the advertised URL to HubExternalURL, so this\nneeds no configuration at all; explicit settings still win.\n\nAuthorization is one structural comparison: the workspace the caller''s\nServiceAccount token was minted in must be the workspace holding the\nAPIExport. A virtual workspace exists for its export''s OWNER to observe\nevery consumer, and a provider''s ServiceAccount lives in the same\nworkspace as the APIExport its `init` created. Nothing is looked up, so\nnothing can be raced or stale. This deliberately does not reuse the\nmembership authorizer — that answers \"may this user read this workspace\",\nand no amount of consumer membership implies the right to read every\nconsumer at once. kcp still applies its own RBAC to the relayed request,\nso the hub narrows rather than replaces it.\n\nThe branch sits ahead of the general ServiceAccount path, which would\notherwise prepend /clusters/{name} and destroy a URL whose tail is kcp''s\nto route. Responses are unbuffered: this traffic is overwhelmingly\nlong-lived watches, and a buffered event would strand a provider until\nthe next write.\n\nThis does NOT generalize to multiple shards, and the docs say so. A\nmulti-shard endpoint slice carries one endpoint per shard, each from that\nshard''s own virtualWorkspaceURL, and nothing in the request distinguishes\nthem — the cluster segment names the APIExport''s workspace, not the shard\nserving it. SaaS gives each shard its own reachable address; kcp''s front\nproxy already serves /services/ and enforces export ownership natively.\n\nTests cover the parser (traversal, empty segments, prefix collisions, the\nescaped wildcard client-go actually sends) and drive the real handler for\nthe isolation property, asserting a denied request never reaches kcp at\nall. Verified they fail without the check.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Ask the self-hoster for the exposure config only they can supply\n\nPublished apps are reached through the tenant''s OWN ingress. The platform\nhas no route into their cluster, so it cannot supply the DNS zone or the\nGateway the generated HTTPRoutes attach to — but the recipe named neither,\nso the rendered command looked complete while quietly leaving app\npublishing disabled.\n\nDeclare the three tenant-owned values with no value, which the renderer\nalready turns into a visible <value> placeholder, a warning, and a row\nunder the portal''s \"You need to fill in\". Omitting them stays valid:\ntemplates that publish an app then fail their reconcile with a clear\nmessage and everything else still provisions — the descriptions say so.\n\nTwo more the platform CAN answer, so it should rather than push a lookup\nonto the user: publishing.hubPublicURL is the hub''s own public address\n({{hubURL}}), and publishing.accessProxyImage is carried from the\nplatform''s own chart value. That last one matters beyond convenience —\nthe gate speaks the hub''s app-access protocol, so a self-hosted copy\nrunning a different build can fail against this hub.\n\nDumping the rendered command exposed two defects in the renderer itself.\nIt force-injects three values chosen when every recipe used the bootstrap\nflow, so a recipe naming one of them got it emitted TWICE (helm takes the\nlast, which is a coin toss on ordering), and a recipe using operator mode\nstill carried bootstrap-only flags. Make those hub values defaults a\nrecipe can override by naming: the recipe describes its own install mode\nand knows better. Defaults it does not name still come through.\n\nWith precedence in place the recipe can now set catalogEntry.enabled\nfalse, which is the correct value for operator mode and was previously\nunsettable: the operator registers the provider from its own embedded\nmanifest, so the chart must not also render a CatalogEntry ConfigMap —\nthat copy is applied by the init container this mode does not run.\n\nTests cover both halves: tenant-owned values reach the user as\nplaceholder AND warning AND unresolved row, platform-owned ones are\nfilled, and a named value appears exactly once. Verified the precedence\ntest fails without the fix — catalogEntry.enabled appears twice.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Do not point the shard''s virtual-workspace URL at the hub\n\nDefaulting Shard.spec.virtualWorkspaceURL to HubExternalURL for embedded\nkcp broke the hub''s own controllers, and did it silently.\n\nThat field is a SINGLE global value feeding every APIExportEndpointSlice,\nand the hub''s own multicluster managers are among its consumers: they\ndial those endpoints in-process using kcp''s CA and kcp''s admin token.\nPointed at the hub they fail the TLS handshake against the hub''s own\nserving certificate —\n\n  http: TLS handshake error from 127.0.0.1:58754:\n  remote error: tls: unknown certificate\n\n— so the CatalogEntry informer never syncs and the provider registry\nfreezes at whatever it last saw. Nothing reports an error: providers\nstill list, the portal still renders install instructions, and every\nrecipe edit is simply ignored. Observed exactly that on a dev hub, where\nonly the providers.faros.sh slice had picked up the new URL: the catalog\nreconciler was the one manager wired to that export, so it was the one\nthat went stale.\n\nThe relay itself is fine and stays. What is missing is any way to\nadvertise it: the hub''s own controllers need a kcp-direct URL while\nexternal consumers need the public one, and one global field cannot\nexpress both. Setting it by hand fails identically, so this is a\nproperty of the field rather than of the default — the docs now say so\ninstead of claiming single-shard platforms need no configuration.\n\nClosing it needs an endpoint override on the multicluster provider, or a\nper-consumer view of the slice. Until then the relay is unreachable and\nsingle-shard platforms are in the same position as multi-shard ones.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1344d78e454745a73f25e805e9f587ff13da78f8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/1344d78e454745a73f25e805e9f587ff13da78f8"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/58ed4d8c1c085cfd4e8d8f8b143ff9d1d103a3da","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users