mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/7698b09bc4ee3998a84c5fa1c46478c548338049/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T12:28:08Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T12:28:08Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Point self-hosting at operator mode, the production install path (#544)\n\nThe self-hosting recipe added in #541 turned on the bootstrap.* init\ncontainer. That is the wrong mode: the chart''s own values.yaml says\noperator mode \"supersedes the bootstrap.* init-container flow\", and\ndocs/application-template-architecture.md says of operator mode \"this is\nthe mode the production install runs in\".\n\nChoosing the superseded flow is why a self-hosted provider stalls with\nevery template failing:\n\n  backend \"kro\" SetupTemplate: get: resourcegraphdefinitions.kro.run\n  \"cron-job\" is forbidden: User \"system:serviceaccount:...\" cannot get\n  resource \"resourcegraphdefinitions\" in API group \"kro.run\"\n\nIn bootstrap mode the chart renders no RBAC at all — ConfigMap,\nDeployment, Service, ServiceAccount and nothing else — so the serve pod\nhas zero permissions on the runtime cluster it is supposed to drive. It\nalso assumes kro is already installed there. Operator mode does both:\nEnsureKroChartCRDs/EnsureKroRelease install kro, and ensureServeRBAC\ngrants the serve ServiceAccount its runtime access. Those are exactly\nthe two things a self-hoster cannot be expected to have done already, so\nbootstrap mode can never stand a provider up unattended.\n\nSwitch the recipe to operator.enabled with the provider kubeconfig\nSecret reference and hub.url. Omitting runtimeKubeconfigSecret makes the\noperator use its own cluster as the runtime, which is what self-hosting\nwants, and the CatalogEntry manifest is embedded in the operator binary,\nso no ConfigMap plumbing is needed either.\n\nNo renderer change was required: hubKnownValue already resolved\noperator.providerKubeconfigSecret.name/key, and every placeholder used\nhere was already supported.\n\nmanifest.yaml and the chart''s catalogentry.yaml are kept in lockstep as\ntheir comments require. Verified the recipe renders a complete install:\nClusterRole, two ClusterRoleBindings, the operator Deployment, and an\nInfrastructureProvider CR with an in-cluster runtime.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a33bda776377f74a9eedac33785bd14e4bc92bac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/a33bda776377f74a9eedac33785bd14e4bc92bac"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/7698b09bc4ee3998a84c5fa1c46478c548338049","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/7698b09bc4ee3998a84c5fa1c46478c548338049","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKDc2OThiMDliYzRlZTM5OThhODRjNWZhMWM0NjQ3OGM1NDgzMzgwNDk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7","sha":"7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7"}],"sha":"7698b09bc4ee3998a84c5fa1c46478c548338049","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/7698b09bc4ee3998a84c5fa1c46478c548338049"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T11:38:14Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T11:38:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Fix two blockers in the self-hosted infrastructure provider (#543)\n\n* Stop the provider charts requiring a heartbeat Secret nobody creates\n\nSelf-hosting the infrastructure provider wedges the pod before it ever\nstarts:\n\n  Error: secret \"faros-infrastructure-hub-token\" not found\n\nThe chart defaulted hub.tokenSecretRef.name to that Secret but never\ncreates it, and the env var is a plain secretKeyRef with no\noptional: true — so kubelet refuses to build the container until it\nexists. The token is not even required: runHeartbeat only sets the\nAuthorization header when non-empty, and the heartbeat endpoint is\nregistered without auth middleware.\n\nIt worked on the dev path only because the Makefile creates the Secret\nby hand first; self-hosting has no equivalent step.\n\ninfrastructure, code, and databricks were the three charts carrying a\nnon-empty default — the other six already defaulted to \"\". Bring those\nthree in line and document why the field is dangerous to set casually.\n\nBoth code install recipes and one infrastructure recipe set that name\nwithout ever creating the Secret, so following them verbatim reproduced\nthe same failure; drop the flag. The dev target genuinely wants the\ntoken and was relying on the default, so it now sets the name\nexplicitly, right below the step that creates the Secret.\n\nVerified all three charts render zero FAROS_HUB_TOKEN references with\ndefaults, that an explicit name still renders the secretKeyRef, and\nthat all three lint clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Point the infrastructure serve container at kcp, not the host cluster\n\nA self-hosted infrastructure provider fails leader election with:\n\n  leases.coordination.k8s.io \"infrastructure-instance\" is forbidden:\n  User \"system:serviceaccount:faros-provider-infrastructure:...\"\n  cannot get resource \"leases\" in the namespace \"default\"\n\nThe identity in that error is a HOST-cluster ServiceAccount, which is\nthe tell: the Lease is supposed to live in the provider''s own kcp\nworkspace, where kcp serves Leases in every logical cluster and the\nminted identity already holds get/list/watch/create/update/patch on\nthem (install/identity.go). Nothing about the lease is misconfigured —\nthe controllers were simply talking to the wrong API server.\n\nloadControllerConfigRaw consulted INFRASTRUCTURE_KUBECONFIG,\nINFRASTRUCTURE_CONTROLLER_KUBECONFIG and KUBECONFIG. The chart sets\nnone of those on the serve container: it sets\nFAROS_PROVIDER_KUBECONFIG, the name standardized across providers and\nthe one the other eight all read. INFRASTRUCTURE_KUBECONFIG is set only\non the init container, and to a path in that container''s own /tmp. So\nserve found nothing and fell through to rest.InClusterConfig().\n\nRead the standardized name first, keeping the provider-specific names\nas fallbacks — the operator sets INFRASTRUCTURE_KUBECONFIG and must\nkeep working. The local `make run-provider-infrastructure` flow sets it\ntoo, which is why this only ever bit the chart-deployed path.\n\nAlso report which source won, and warn loudly when it is the in-cluster\nServiceAccount. Every controller and both elections share this config,\nso choosing wrong misdirects the entire provider and the symptom\nsurfaces far from the cause — as it did here.\n\nTests cover the regression, the operator''s env, precedence, and the\ndisabled sentinel. Verified they fail without the fix: the standardized\ncase resolves to the operator''s kubeconfig, or to nothing at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f97cca161c1df1c2ddc5d9207844b32d8dbcbcb3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/f97cca161c1df1c2ddc5d9207844b32d8dbcbcb3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKDdiZjU0YWNkNzE5YTJjNDc0MjRkNmQ4NWU5ZjhmM2U0NjRjOGRmZTc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae","sha":"64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae"}],"sha":"7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/7bf54acd719a2c47424d6d85e9f8f3e464c8dfe7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T10:52:31Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T10:52:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Stop the provider charts requiring a heartbeat Secret nobody creates (#542)\n\nSelf-hosting the infrastructure provider wedges the pod before it ever\nstarts:\n\n  Error: secret \"faros-infrastructure-hub-token\" not found\n\nThe chart defaulted hub.tokenSecretRef.name to that Secret but never\ncreates it, and the env var is a plain secretKeyRef with no\noptional: true — so kubelet refuses to build the container until it\nexists. The token is not even required: runHeartbeat only sets the\nAuthorization header when non-empty, and the heartbeat endpoint is\nregistered without auth middleware.\n\nIt worked on the dev path only because the Makefile creates the Secret\nby hand first; self-hosting has no equivalent step.\n\ninfrastructure, code, and databricks were the three charts carrying a\nnon-empty default — the other six already defaulted to \"\". Bring those\nthree in line and document why the field is dangerous to set casually.\n\nBoth code install recipes and one infrastructure recipe set that name\nwithout ever creating the Secret, so following them verbatim reproduced\nthe same failure; drop the flag. The dev target genuinely wants the\ntoken and was relying on the default, so it now sets the name\nexplicitly, right below the step that creates the Secret.\n\nVerified all three charts render zero FAROS_HUB_TOKEN references with\ndefaults, that an explicit name still renders the secretKeyRef, and\nthat all three lint clean.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a9d890cd3e27f61b208e81672ba8d698271d4312","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/a9d890cd3e27f61b208e81672ba8d698271d4312"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKDY0MzU4ZWI2MzJmZDY2ZmU2NWY1NDgxYmFjOTU1NGYzZGY1NjQ3YWU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac","sha":"bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac"}],"sha":"64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/64358eb632fd66fe65f5481bac9554f3df5647ae"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T10:24:59Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T10:24:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Org-owned providers, self-hosting, and a scripted install flow (#541)\n\n* Let organizations run providers themselves\n\nAdds \"bring your own\" providers: an organization can register a provider it\nruns in its own cluster, and self-host any platform provider that publishes a\ndeployment recipe. The hub creates a workspace for it, mints a credential\nscoped to that workspace alone, and generates the Helm commands to install it.\n\nWorkspace layout mirrors the platform one a level down, at\nroot:faros:tenants:<org>:providers:<name>. The well-known `providers` container\nis a plain `universal` workspace, which is what lets each org provider reuse the\nexisting `provider` WorkspaceType — so no new WorkspaceType ships here, the\nprovider install path runs unmodified, and org providers join the existing\nCatalogEntry watch with no change to the catalog manager.\n\nOwnership is derived from the workspace path (the LogicalCluster''s kcp.io/path),\nnever from a field on the CatalogEntry, which the provider''s own init writes.\nThat read uses the hub''s kcp-admin config rather than the reconcile request''s\nclient: the latter is scoped to the providers.faros.sh virtual workspace, which\nserves only what its APIExport declares, so LogicalCluster is unreachable there.\nA failed resolution fails the reconcile rather than defaulting, because the\ndefault scope is platform-global — the widest one.\n\nIsolation: the registry is keyed by (orgUUID, name), and Registry.Get stays\nplatform-only so an org copy can never capture a platform provider''s proxy or\nheartbeat route by name. Only the tenant-scoped lookups see it. Enable is\nhub-mediated and resolves through GetForOrg. The kcp `bind` verb is NOT yet\norg-scoped — see docs/byo-providers.md for that gap.\n\nSelf-hosting metadata lives in CatalogEntry.spec.selfHosting: chart coordinates\nplus the values the installer must supply. The hub fills in what it knows\nauthoritatively (its own URL, the kubeconfig Secret, identity hashes resolved\nper organization) so the generated command is paste-ready; anything it cannot\nresolve is flagged rather than silently rendered as a working value. Charts\nembed their own README into the entry, so the portal shows the values reference\nfor the version actually deployed, offline.\n\nprovider-sdk: WorkspacePath is now optional. It was hardcoded to the platform\npath and written into APIExportEndpointSlice.spec.export.path, so a chart\ninstalled against an org workspace published endpoints for an export that does\nnot exist there. kcp resolves an unset path to the slice''s own cluster, which is\nalways where the export is, so the field was duplicating what the kubeconfig\nalready carries. Existing slices carrying an explicit path are left alone —\nspec.export is immutable, so rewriting them means delete-and-recreate, briefly\ndropping virtual-workspace endpoints for no gain.\n\nAlso fixes the org `providers` container leaking into tenant workspace lists and\ninto the kcp proxy''s authorizer, which would have granted every org member\naccess to the workspace that parents provider credentials.\n\nEvery provider now ships a README and a chart values reference.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Script the install flow and cover both kcp topologies in e2e\n\nInstallation is now scripted, and the scripts are the single source of truth\nshared by the docs and the e2e suites: the docs quote them step by step and the\nsuites execute them verbatim, so a drift between \"what we tell people to run\"\nand \"what we test\" becomes a test failure rather than a stale page.\n\nhack/install/ carries the ordered steps (kind, cert-manager, envoy gateway with\nTLS passthrough, etcd, kcp-operator, shards, hub) plus lib.sh holding the whole\nenv-var contract, and teardown/port-forward helpers.\n\nTwo topologies are documented and tested: embedded kcp (single hub StatefulSet,\nkcp etcd on a PVC) and external multi-shard kcp (front-proxy, two shards on a\nshared etcd, N hub replicas). Both serve the same tenancy plane, which the\nshared org/workspace CRUD cases assert.\n\ntest/e2e/framework/install.go drives the same scripts from Go rather than\nreimplementing any step — fix the script and the doc, not the harness.\n\nAlso adds .faros-install/ to .gitignore (script state lives there).\n\nIncludes providers/secrets/portal/dist, which has no Go source, manifest, or\nassets.go embedding it yet.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep GET /api/providers reachable when identity cannot be resolved\n\nOrg scoping put the catalog behind tenant.OptionalMiddleware, which rejected\nthe request when the caller could not be resolved. That turned a previously\nunauthenticated endpoint into one that 500s on any user-lookup error, and the\nprovider e2e suite caught it: the static-token resolver''s user lookup fails in\nthat harness, so /api/providers answered 500 instead of the catalog.\n\nMake the middleware genuinely optional. It now never rejects — a missing\ncredential, an unresolvable one, a backend error during the lookup, or an Org\nthe caller has no membership for all fall through with that much less context\nattached.\n\nThat is safe because of the direction of the failure: attaching context can only\never ADD to what a handler shows, so dropping it shows less, never more.\nHandlers behind it already treat an empty OrgUUID as \"global scope only\", and\nanything that must not be served anonymously belongs behind Middleware instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Pin org isolation of the provider catalog with tests\n\nThe catalog endpoint is unauthenticated — it is registered on the root router\nahead of the authenticated /api subrouter, so the platform catalog is readable\nby anyone who can reach the hub. That predates provider scoping, but a comment\non assistantSkills asserted the opposite (\"the authenticated /api/providers\nresponse\"), which is the kind of claim that quietly licenses putting something\nsensitive in the payload. Corrected it, and pointed at the fix if the platform\ncatalog should not be public: move the route behind the authenticated\nsubrouter, rather than relaxing the org check.\n\nThe org-scoped half is the part that must hold regardless, so it now has tests\nthat wire the real handler behind the real middleware: an anonymous caller, a\ncaller whose identity cannot be resolved, and an authenticated member of\nanother org who spoofs X-Faros-Org all see the platform catalog only. A\nverified member sees their own org''s providers, so those three cannot pass by\nthe handler simply never returning org entries.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Require authentication to read the provider catalog\n\nGET /api/providers was reachable by anyone who could reach the hub: it is\nregistered on the root router, ahead of the authenticated /api subrouter, so no\nmiddleware ever ran. That let the platform catalog — provider names,\ndescriptions, permission claims, action schemas, and inline assistant-skill\nbodies — be enumerated anonymously. Org-owned entries were never exposed (those\nneed a membership verified against the caller''s UserMembershipIndex), but the\nplatform catalog describes what a deployment runs and should not be scrapeable.\n\nThe endpoint now requires an authenticated caller. The Org stays optional,\nbecause the portal fetches the catalog before one is selected; the identity does\nnot. The portal already gates its fetch on auth.isAuthenticated, so the shell is\nunaffected.\n\nGetting this right needed a distinction the resolver could not previously make.\n\"No bearer\" was a sentinel, but every later failure — an unverifiable token and\na valid token whose User record could not be read — collapsed into one generic\nerror. Treating that as authenticated would admit forged tokens; treating it as\nunauthenticated would refuse valid callers whenever the user store hiccups, which\nis exactly what broke the provider e2e. So post-verification failures now wrap\nErrUserRecordUnavailable, and the middleware splits them:\n\n  no credential / unverifiable  -> 401\n  accepted, record unreadable   -> served, with NO tenant context\n  resolved                      -> context, Org attached only once verified\n\nRenames OptionalMiddleware to OptionalOrgMiddleware: it is the Org that is\noptional now, not the caller.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Stop the template e2e failing on a readiness wait it cannot satisfy\n\n#540 added a readyWhen to the httpRoute node of the application and\nsimple-webapp templates: the route must be Accepted by the platform Gateway.\n`make e2e-infrastructure-up` installs the Gateway API CRDs but no Gateway\ncontroller, so nothing ever writes HTTPRoute.status.parents and the route can\nnever be Accepted there. Both templates have failed on main since.\n\nThe suite already scopes itself to \"the RGD is accepted and its objects get\napplied\", and says so — readiness is out of scope because the throwaway kind\ncluster cannot satisfy it (images may not even pull). The assumption that broke\nis the one in the comment above the markers: that readiness waits do not contain\napply-error substrings. kro nests the wait inside its apply-results message:\n\n  resource reconciliation failed: apply results contain errors: waiting for\n  node \"httpRoute\": ... failed to evaluate readyWhen expression: ...\n  (waiting for readiness)\n\nso \"apply results contain errors\" matched even though nothing failed to apply.\n\nReadiness waits are now recognised and skipped before the apply-error check.\nReal failures are untouched: an invalid object, a Required-value rejection, and\nan admission-webhook denial all still fail, and the separate assertion that kro\nactually created the child objects still runs — simple-webapp continues to\nverify HTTPRoute×1 exists, so this only relaxes \"is it Ready\", not \"was it\napplied\".\n\nVerified with `make e2e-infrastructure`: all 8 seed templates pass, including\nthe two that were failing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Narrow the readiness-wait match to kro''s explicit waiting phrase\n\nThe first cut also matched \"readyWhen\" and \"waiting for node\". Both are too\nbroad: a readyWhen that fails to COMPILE is a real template bug and its message\nmentions readyWhen too, so those markers would have silently passed exactly the\ndefect this suite exists to catch.\n\nMatch only \"waiting for readiness\" — the phrase kro appends when it is waiting\nrather than failing. If kro ever reports a wait without it, the test fails\nloudly instead of skipping something it should not, which is the safe direction\nfor a guard.\n\nRe-verified with `make e2e-infrastructure`: all 8 templates still pass, and a\nsimulated CEL compile failure in a readyWhen is still reported as an error.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Read kro''s instance state instead of grepping its messages, and stand up a gateway\n\nReplaces both halves of the earlier workaround.\n\nStructural signal. kro publishes progress in status.state, so the suite reads\nthat rather than matching substrings in condition prose:\n\n  ERROR / FAILED  a node failed — an invalid object, a Required value, a\n                  webhook rejection, or a readyWhen that cannot be evaluated\n  IN_PROGRESS     everything applied; some node has not converged yet\n  ACTIVE          every node synced and ready\n\n(kro''s StateManager.Update: any node in Error makes the instance ERROR,\nall-synced makes it ACTIVE, everything else — including WaitingForReadiness —\nleaves it IN_PROGRESS.)\n\nSubstrings could not express that. kro nests a readiness wait inside its\n\"apply results contain errors\" text, so a wait and a real failure read alike,\nand the marker that told them apart (\"readyWhen\") also matched a readyWhen that\nfails to COMPILE — a genuine template bug the suite would then have passed.\nFailures now print the instance''s conditions, so a broken template says why\nrather than just \"ERROR\".\n\nReal readiness. simulateGatewayController accepts this instance''s HTTPRoutes\nthe way a Gateway implementation would — Accepted + ResolvedRefs, True, stamped\nwith the route''s current generation, under a parentRef matching the configured\nGateway — re-stamping as generations change. `make e2e-infrastructure-up`\ninstalls the Gateway API CRDs but no controller, so without it status.parents\nstays empty and the exposure layer''s readyWhen CEL is never evaluated against a\npopulated status. With it, every seed template reaches ACTIVE, so the expression\nis exercised rather than tolerated. IN_PROGRESS is still accepted after a short\ngrace, since a kind cluster cannot make every node ready.\n\nVerified against a tree merged with main (this branch predates #540, so the\nreadyWhen is absent here and a local run would prove nothing):\n\n  - all 8 seed templates + all 3 dev-overlay cases reach ACTIVE;\n  - a readyWhen referencing an unknown identifier fails;\n  - a Service port of the wrong type fails;\n  - dev-overlay wall time back to ~28s from ~496s.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8ed79b54758cf011bac26f5d098ad89d22142b3e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/8ed79b54758cf011bac26f5d098ad89d22142b3e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKGJiMDEzZjZhZTY0YmFhMGYwYjVhOWUyMDJkMzAwMDRmYzE0ZDZjYWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9","sha":"02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9"}],"sha":"bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/bb013f6ae64baa0f0b5a9e202d30004fc14d6cac"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5685031?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/cwilhit","id":5685031,"login":"cwilhit","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2ODUwMzE=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-19T01:46:54Z","email":"cdwilhite0601@gmail.com","name":"Craig D Wilhite"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-19T01:46:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Polish App Studio publishing, resilience, and preview workflows (#540)\n\n* Add App Studio deployment history and rollback\n\n* Improve release artifact verification and launcher tab selection\n\n* Guard deployment on stale production status and loading state\n\n* Add project history restoration and expose source revision\n\n* Simplify provider portal UI and remove obsolete code\n\n* Gate app previews on Envoy Gateway readiness","tree":{"sha":"caee8d2470e613922831c2407c8514ccfe5a30cd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/caee8d2470e613922831c2407c8514ccfe5a30cd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKDAyYzU1MmFkMTM1ZmY0OTEyNjE4YzAwYzkyZjBkYjgxOTI0YTM4Zjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001","sha":"d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001"}],"sha":"02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/02c552ad135ff4912618c00c92f0db81924a38f9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-18T04:38:34Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-18T04:38:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Scale every provider: leader election, claims, tunnel routing, and an in-cluster dev loop (#531)\n\n* Add leader election to provider controllers\n\nProviders ran every controller on every replica: the only election in the\ntree was the hub''s, while three provider charts defaulted to two replicas —\ndelivering active-active managers by accident and constant \"object has been\nmodified\" conflict churn on the infrastructure provider.\n\nPort the hub''s election loop to provider-sdk/leaderelection (Lease in the\nprovider''s own kcp workspace, so the hub-minted kubeconfig is the only\ncredential involved) and gate every singleton write loop on it:\n\n- infrastructure serve: Template manager, Instance controller, and the\n  bootstrap install loop each hold their own lease; managers are rebuilt\n  fresh per term with SkipNameValidation (controller names register\n  process-globally).\n- code and vibe-studio: whole multicluster manager per lease, making\n  vibe-studio''s two-replica chart default legitimate.\n- infrastructure CRD operator: controller-runtime''s built-in Lease election\n  on the runtime cluster, with chart RBAC for leases + election events and\n  the minted workspace SA granted coordination.k8s.io/leases.\n\nNon-leaders keep serving REST/MCP/portal traffic throughout; only the write\nloops are gated.\n\nedges, databricks, kuery, and app-studio are deliberately NOT gated: their\nmanagers double as serving machinery (tunnel tenant resolver, HTTP action\nauthority, per-replica sync-engine engagement, pod-local workspace store),\nso a lease would break non-leader serving. Each now documents why and what\nmust change before scaling past one replica.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Plan horizontal scaling for the four replica-pinned providers\n\nLeader election covered code/vibe-studio/infrastructure; edges, databricks,\nkuery, and app-studio stayed pinned to one replica because their managers\ndouble as serving machinery. Audit all four plus the hub''s routing/session\nlayer, and record the design:\n\n- No hub-level session/affinity layer: three of the four scale without any\n  hub change, and state placement (not request placement) is the real\n  problem. The hub keeps its \"no request pinned to a pod\" model.\n- Three provider-sdk primitives instead: a sharedstore port (Secrets KV in\n  the provider''s own workspace), an ownership-claims registry (leader\n  election generalized to N items), and headless-Service peer forwarding.\n- Per-provider: databricks needs only a slice-backed cluster authority\n  (agents provider already proves the pattern); kuery needs its dead sync\n  path revived, Postgres, and claims-partitioned engagement; edges adopts\n  tunnel-per-replica (Konnectivity model, per the in-repo prior-art survey);\n  app-studio gets durable run ownership in Postgres and replica-pinned\n  projects with git re-hydration as the crash story.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Scale the databricks provider: slice-backed authority + leader-elected controllers\n\nThe HTTP action path resolved tenant clusters through the RUNNING\nmulticluster manager (managerAuthority), which coupled serving, readiness,\nand heartbeat eligibility to controller lifecycle and pinned the provider to\none replica.\n\nReplace the manager-backed authority with tenant.SliceAuthority: tenant\nclients are built directly from the APIExport virtual-workspace URLs on the\nendpoint slice (multi-shard aware, probe + learned shard cache — the same\npattern the agents provider already runs), authenticated with the provider\ncredential. Serving now works on every replica with no warm-up and no\ncontroller dependency, so:\n\n- /readyz and heartbeats gate on serving readiness (factory configured +\n  slice discovered) only; controller state is reported for observability.\n- The controller manager is leader-elected (databricks-controllers Lease in\n  the provider workspace) and rebuilt fresh per term; the election loop\n  replaces the bespoke retry/readiness machinery (transport-readiness\n  round-tripper, provider runnable, bounded setup, retry gate — ~600 lines\n  deleted). A slim per-term watchdog still stops the manager when the\n  endpoint slice loses its last usable URL.\n\nTrade-offs recorded in tenant/authority.go: authority reads are live GETs\ninstead of informer-cached, and an unbound tenant now gets kcp''s 403/404\ninstead of \"cluster unavailable\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Scale the kuery provider: revive edge sync and shard engagement with per-edge claims\n\nThe engagement data path was dead three ways (tracked in\ncross-provider-simplification.md): it watched the retired faros.sh/v1alpha1\nEdge kind, claimed that kind on its APIExport, and dialed the hub''s removed\n/services/edges-proxy mount. And even alive, engagement was per-replica\nserving state that pinned the chart to one replica.\n\nRevive and shard it:\n\n- Watch edges.faros.sh/v1alpha1 KubernetesCluster (LinuxServer edges carry\n  no Kubernetes API and are no longer claimed) and dial the edges provider''s\n  consumer proxy /services/providers/edges/edgeproxy/.../kubernetesclusters/\n  {name}/k8s — the Enable-time edge-proxy grant already covers the new kinds\n  with the proxy verb.\n- Shard engagement across replicas with one Lease per edge in the provider\n  workspace (claims.go): try-acquire/renew/release, takeover after a 60s\n  TTL, released early when an edge disconnects or is deleted. The owning\n  replica''s reconcile requeues every 20s to renew the claim AND re-assert\n  the kuery cluster row (active + tenant label), which also heals the\n  transient markStale a previous owner''s disengage leaves behind.\n- Serve /api/edges from the shared store (active rows carrying the tenant\n  label) instead of the in-process engagement map, so any replica lists the\n  full fleet; EngagedCount stays per-replica for /api/status introspection.\n- Chart: refuse replicaCount > 1 with the default SQLite store — scaling\n  requires store.driver=postgres, since per-pod SQLite means per-pod data.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Scale the edges provider: single-dial agents + replica-routed tunnels\n\nThe edges provider was pinned to one replica because revdial registers\ntunnel dialers in a process-global map that closes over the accepted socket:\nan agent''s control connection, every later pickup connection, and every\nconsumer request had to reach the same process.\n\nMake it horizontally scalable WITHOUT agents dialing every replica — each\nagent keeps exactly one control connection, and the provider routes to the\ntunnel''s owner internally:\n\n- Tunnel ownership registry (internal/tunnel/registry.go): the replica\n  terminating a tunnel claims a per-edge Lease in the provider workspace\n  (holder = its podIP:internalPort relay address) plus a presence Lease per\n  replica; claims are renewed by the ConnManager sweeper and expire in 90s\n  when a replica dies. Reconnects overwrite unconditionally — the live\n  socket is ground truth — and releases are owner-checked so a slow\n  disconnect cleanup cannot erase a newer claim.\n\n- Relay plane (internal/tunnel/remote.go): ConnManager.Load returns the\n  local revdial dialer or, for peer-held tunnels, a remoteDialer that opens\n  a raw byte stream to the owner''s internal listener (HTTP 101 upgrade,\n  authenticated with the shared provider bearer; the port is pod-to-pod\n  only, never on the Service). Every consumer — kubectl/exec/port-forward,\n  SSH, service proxy, MCP tools, discovery/validation reconcilers, event\n  subscribers — works unchanged from any replica.\n\n- Replica-addressed pickups: dialers advertise their pickup path as\n  /agent/proxy/{replicaID}; whichever replica the Service hands the pickup\n  to forwards the WebSocket upgrade to the owner, resolved via its presence\n  Lease. The agent treats the pickup path as opaque — zero agent changes.\n\n- HasConnection/Keys are now fleet-wide, which also fixes the multi-replica\n  status flap the lifecycle reconciler would have caused, and event\n  subscribers are gated to the tunnel-owning replica so N replicas don''t\n  open N sessions per service.\n\nChart: POD_NAME/POD_IP downward API, internal port 8090, RollingUpdate\n(maxUnavailable 0) instead of Recreate — a rollout only disconnects the\nreplaced pod''s agents, which reconnect to a survivor. Without POD_IP or a\nbearer credential the provider runs in the historical single-replica mode.\n\nKnown gap (documented in-code): UniFi event buffers stay owner-local until\nthe events store moves to a shared backend, and the controllers remain\nactive-active until the serving path gets a slice-backed tenant resolver.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Design replica awareness for app-studio\n\nRun claims in Postgres (cluster-wide Busy/reservations, orphan-interrupt on\nclaim expiry instead of local-map absence) plus project pinning with peer\nforwarding (lazy per-project claims carrying the owner''s pod address, an\ninternal HTTP forwarding listener, git re-hydration on claim moves so the\nworkspace volume becomes an emptyDir cache). Reconcilers shard by project\nclaim rather than leader-electing — the work is partitioned, not singleton.\nNo hub changes; the Service keeps round-robining and the provider forwards\ninternally, same shape as the edges tunnel routing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* App Studio replica awareness, phases A+B: durable run claims and project affinity\n\nPhase A — run ownership moves from process memory to Postgres\n(app_studio_replica_claims, one activity claim per project scope):\n\n- The supervisor claims a project''s activity on Attach (run ID) and Reserve\n  (\"operation\"), heartbeats every live claim on a 15s loop, and releases on\n  finish/shutdown — owner-checked, so a slow cleanup cannot erase a newer\n  claim, and reservation release defers to a run that attached meanwhile.\n- AssistantBusy consults the durable claim after the local maps, so the\n  commit-convergence gate and external-operation locks are cluster-wide\n  correct; an unreadable store fails busy (never commit on uncertainty).\n- The orphan-run reconciler no longer interrupts \"runs not in my local map\":\n  only runs whose claim is missing, expired (the owner died), or self-owned\n  without a local run (a detach leftover) are orphaned. A fresh foreign\n  claim means a worker is heartbeating the run on a peer. This deletes the\n  cross-replica run kill (audit F1) and improves single-replica restart\n  semantics for free.\n\nPhase B — project affinity + peer forwarding (docs/app-studio-replica-awareness.md):\n\n- Projects are lazily pinned to the first replica that touches their\n  workspace (project claim carrying podIP:internalPort, 10-minute idle\n  expiry). A middleware on project-scoped routes serves owned projects,\n  claims unowned ones, and reverse-proxies the rest to the owner''s internal\n  listener — store/CR-backed GETs (SSE streams, listings) serve anywhere,\n  workspace-reading GETs (files, skills) follow the pin, unknown routes\n  default to forwarding.\n- The internal listener (APP_STUDIO_INTERNAL_PORT, pod-to-pod only, shared\n  provider-bearer auth) marks forwarded requests with a loop-guard header\n  the public listener strips.\n- The workspace source-revision fence is mirrored onto the project claim on\n  every dev-sync, so the sandbox staleness check survives a future\n  claim-driven move (Phase C seeds it back on hydration).\n\nSingle-replica behavior is unchanged (every claim resolves locally and\nforwarding never triggers); the chart keeps replicaCount pinned at 1 until\nPhase C lands claim-driven workspace re-hydration.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* App Studio replica awareness, phase C: adoption, ephemeral workspaces, scaling unlocked\n\nThe workspace tree becomes a CACHE of git plus in-flight edits, which is\nwhat lets replicas scale:\n\n- Adoption: when a replica acquires a project claim previously held by\n  another replica (or by nobody), it seeds the source-revision fence from\n  the claim''s durable floor and rebuilds the workspace from the last commit\n  via the existing hydrate flow, as the caller. A same-pod process restart\n  (previous owner has this replica''s own forwarding address) keeps the\n  local volume — it may hold uncommitted work newer than git. Projects\n  without a repository skip hydration; hydration failures serve with the\n  local tree and log loudly rather than bricking the project.\n- The supervisor''s renewal loop also heartbeats the project PIN of every\n  live run, so a long assistant turn with no HTTP traffic cannot have its\n  project adopted mid-write.\n- Commit convergence is owner-gated (Reconciler.Owns): every replica still\n  runs the Project reconciler, but a stale tree left behind on a previous\n  owner is never committed over the live owner''s work. No live claim means\n  any replica may converge — only the one with leftover dirty files finds\n  anything to commit. An unreadable store refuses (never commit on\n  uncertainty).\n- Chart: workspace defaults to emptyDir (pods are cattle; committed work is\n  always safe in git), RollingUpdate with maxUnavailable 0 in emptyDir\n  mode, and the replicaCount hard-fail is now conditional — only PVC-backed\n  workspaces stay pinned to one replica, because one RWO volume cannot back\n  a fleet.\n\nThe deliberate trade stands as designed: a replica crash loses only that\nreplica''s edits since the last commit; phase D (periodic WIP snapshots to a\nscratch branch) remains optional hardening.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Run second replicas in the Tilt dev loop\n\nExercise the provider scaling machinery during normal development:\n\n- infrastructure-pod (helm into the faros-kro kind cluster) deploys TWO\n  replicas — the only Tilt resource with real kube replicas and Service\n  round-robin, covering the leader-elected Template/Instance controllers\n  and bootstrap loop.\n- New manual `replicas` group with second host-binary instances: code-2\n  (leader election), kuery-2 (per-edge claim sharding over the shared dev\n  Postgres), edges-2 (tunnel-ownership registry + loopback relay). The hub\n  proxies each provider through one fixed URL, so these standbys validate\n  the durable coordination rather than request routing.\n- run-provider-edges now always sets a replica identity\n  (POD_NAME/POD_IP/EDGES_INTERNAL_PORT, loopback defaults) so both edges\n  instances run with replica routing enabled — a routing-less instance\n  would see no tunnels in the registry and flap edge status.\n\nDeliberately absent: a second hub (Tilt runs --embedded-kcp; HA requires\nexternal kcp) and app-studio-2 (dev uses the per-process in-memory message\nstore; replica claims need the shared Postgres store to mean anything).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Run the hub with two replicas in tilt-cluster\n\nTiltfile.cluster deploys the hub chart in external-kcp mode — exactly the\nconfiguration the hub HA work targets — so set replicaCount=2: singleton\ncontrollers gate on the faros-hub-controllers Lease, sessions and\npublished-app auth codes live in kcp-backed Secrets, and in-cluster traffic\n(providers, minted kubeconfigs via hub.internalURL) round-robins across both\npods. Noted at the resource: Tilt''s :9443 port-forward attaches to one pod,\nso browser traffic pins to a single replica — kill that pod to exercise\nfailover from the browser.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Run every provider as pods in tilt-cluster\n\nHost-binary providers could never exercise the in-cluster behavior the\nscaling work exists for — no real replicas, no Service round-robin, no chart\nprobes. Replace all of them with Tilt-built images + each provider''s\nproduction chart in a faros-providers namespace:\n\n- quickstart, code, kuery, databricks, edges, app-studio at replicaCount=2\n  (leader election, slice authority, claim sharding, tunnel routing, run\n  claims + project affinity — all live in dev now); agents at 1.\n- infrastructure switches to the chart''s CRD-driven operator mode: the\n  operator bootstraps the workspace with the synced admin kubeconfig,\n  helm-installs kro into this cluster, owns the 2-replica serve Deployment,\n  and self-registers the CatalogEntry — the host controller + skip-serve\n  split is gone.\n- kcp-dns (new hack/scripts/configure-tilt-kcp-dns.sh): CoreDNS answers\n  (kcp|*.kcp).localhost with the Envoy gateway ClusterIP, cluster-wide —\n  the pod equivalent of the hub''s hostAliases — so pods use the\n  operator-issued kubeconfigs unchanged.\n- <name>-secrets resources sync the init-minted kubeconfigs from .kcp/ into\n  each pod''s Secret; until register+init run, the pod visibly waits on the\n  missing mount. CatalogEntry URLs now rewrite to in-cluster Services\n  instead of host.docker.internal.\n- One shared providers-db Postgres (kuery / appstudio / agents databases)\n  replaces the host docker containers — which also makes app-studio''s\n  replica claims real in dev instead of per-process in-memory.\n- agents chart gains envFromSecret so the .env credential file becomes a\n  synced Secret; app-studio''s hub CA export now also lands in the ConfigMap\n  its chart mounts. previewConsole stays off in pod mode (the dev signing\n  key id is generated at runtime, which chart values cannot consume).\n\nEvery chart renders with the exact values passed, the Tiltfile parses, and\na smoke docker build passes. Ports forward to their historical host ports.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Give providers-db a first-boot grace window on its readiness probe\n\nPostgres''s initdb runs a temporary \"rejecting connections\" server before the\nreal one starts; with no initial delay the probe painted that normal window\nas failures in Tilt. The pod was healthy — this is cosmetic.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Build provider images from the monorepo (in-tree provider-sdk)\n\nProvider images resolved github.com/faroshq/provider-sdk from the module\nproxy at v0.1.0 — which predates the leaderelection package — so every image\nbuild using it broke the moment the scaling work landed (Tilt surfaced it;\nCI provider-release was equally broken). The code provider''s Dockerfile even\ncarried a TODO(sdk-publish) for exactly this.\n\nMake the monorepo the source of truth:\n\n- Every provider go.mod gains `replace github.com/faroshq/provider-sdk =>\n  ../../provider-sdk` (+ go mod tidy), so standalone module builds resolve\n  the in-tree SDK — no publish step in the loop.\n- Dockerfiles build from the REPO ROOT (docker build -f\n  providers/<name>/Dockerfile .), copying provider-sdk next to the module;\n  the same pattern the hub image already uses. provider-release and the\n  Makefile infra image target switch context accordingly; the root\n  .dockerignore additionally drops .git/.kcp/bin/kubeconfigs.\n- vibe-studio''s hand-maintained COPY list had rotted (missing provision/\n  webtools/sessionlog) — switched to whole-module COPY like its peers.\n\nLive-debug fixes from the first tilt-cluster run:\n\n- infrastructure serve pods pulled faros-infrastructure-provider:0.1.0,\n  unreachable: the operator stamps the image from the CR''s split repo/tag\n  fields, which Tilt cannot inject into. custom_build now also tags :dev and\n  kind-loads it, and the CR pins tag=dev. (Rebuilds need a manual rollout\n  restart of the operator-owned serve Deployment.)\n- app-studio''s init hard-fails without the infrastructure/code APIExport\n  identityHashes (kcp rejects hash-less claims): a discovery resource does\n  the same lookups the host init target did and writes a file read_file()\n  watches, so Tilt re-renders the chart the moment discovery succeeds.\n- databricks: numeric runAsUser/runAsGroup (65532) — kubelet refuses\n  runAsNonRoot with distroless''s non-numeric \"nonroot\" USER.\n\nAll nine images build from the root context; the Tiltfile parses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Wire the edges APIExport identityHash into the kuery pod\n\nThe revived kubernetesclusters permission claim is on a non-built-in type,\nso kcp hard-rejects it without the edges APIExport''s identityHash — same\nfailure class app-studio hit with its infrastructure/code claims. Same\npattern: a discovery resource writes .kcp/kuery-edges-identity-hash.env,\nread_file() re-renders the chart the moment it appears.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Route all dev traffic through the hub; give the code pod its .env secrets\n\nAnswering \"why do we need port-forwards in the first place?\" — we don''t.\nEvery real path (portal UI, backend API, MCP) goes hub → in-cluster Service\nvia the CatalogEntry URLs, and the pod-port forwards were both vestigial and\nbroken (several charts hardcode the container PORT, so forwarding the\nservice port hit nothing and flapped with \"lost connection to pod\").\nProvider resources now expose no host forwards; debug directly with\nkubectl -n faros-providers port-forward svc/<name> when needed.\n\nThe portal failure was the same vestige at the registry level: five\nCatalogEntries still pointed at host.docker.internal from pre-pod\nregistrations. Re-registering rewrites them to Service URLs, and the\ninfrastructure manifest rewrite now targets the operator-managed namespace\n(faros-infrastructure-provider) where its serve Service actually lives —\nall eight providers answer 200 through the hub proxy.\n\nThe code chart gains envFromSecret (same shape as agents) and Tilt syncs\nproviders/code/.env into a code-env Secret, so GitHub OAuth app credentials\nreach the pod the way the host binary sourced them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Keep one host forward: the code provider''s GitHub OAuth loop\n\nThe GitHub OAuth flow is deliberately browser → provider DIRECT (never\nthrough the hub''s authenticated proxy), and the OAuth App''s registered\ncallback is http://localhost:8083/oauth/github/callback — so code keeps a\npod forward on 8083 while every other path stays hub → Service. Both OAuth\nlegs (start + callback) ride the same forward to the same pod, so the\nin-process state handshake also holds at two replicas.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Stamp INFRASTRUCTURE_WORKSPACE_PATH onto the operator-owned serve Deployment\n\nIn the supplied-admin flow (spec.providerWorkspace set, root-scoped\nkubeconfig) the operator retargeted the workspace for its OWN bootstrap but\nnever passed the path to serve — whose controllers then watched the root\ncluster, where the platform kinds don''t exist. The symptom chain: Template\nsource \"no matches for kind\", cache sync timing out at exactly the 2-minute\ndefault, the leader-elected manager dying and re-campaigning every term, and\nInstance finalizers never running (deletes hung forever).\n\nProduction never hit this because hub-minted kubeconfigs are already\nworkspace-scoped and leave spec.providerWorkspace empty — the env is only\nstamped when the path is set, keeping that flow untouched. Verified live: a\n10-hour-stuck Instance finalized within ~80s of the restamped serve pods\nstarting.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Do not gate databricks readiness on discovered VW endpoints\n\nVirtual-workspace endpoints only exist once a tenant binds the APIExport —\ngating pod readiness on them made a chicken-and-egg in pod mode: an unready\nprovider has no Service endpoints, the hub 503s it, and no tenant can ever\nEnable it to create the first binding (it also wedged rollouts behind a\nnever-ready pod). Serving readiness now requires configuration plus an\ninstalled authority only; shard discovery stays lazy per request, and a\ntenant action before any binding fails with a clear per-call error. The\ncontroller manager keeps its own endpoint gate — the election loop retries\nuntil a binding exists.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* Move the remaining image builds to repo-root contexts\n\nimages.yaml (the PR-validation/hub-release builder) still built providers\nwith per-provider contexts, so every provider image failed against the new\nroot-context Dockerfiles; and the access-proxy Dockerfile shares the\ninfrastructure go.mod, whose in-tree provider-sdk replace no longer resolves\nin a module-local context — its go mod download broke even with its old\ncontext. Default the images.yaml matrix to the repo root (dev-agent keeps\nits self-contained module-local context explicitly), convert\nDockerfile.access-proxy to root-context paths with the sdk copied in, and\nalign the provider-release access-proxy step.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"022fad6b840aef88f7d85342889d55e9c80d1a28","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/022fad6b840aef88f7d85342889d55e9c80d1a28"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKGQzYjY2ZWVmN2ZhNmYxYTlhODJkMDQ2YjQ0NjFhNDZmOTgwMTYwMDE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11","sha":"84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11"}],"sha":"d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/d3b66eef7fa6f1a9a82d046b4461a46f98016001"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5685031?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/cwilhit","id":5685031,"login":"cwilhit","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU2ODUwMzE=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cwilhit","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-17T22:37:18Z","email":"cdwilhite0601@gmail.com","name":"Craig D Wilhite"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-17T22:37:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Improve App Studio assistant annotations and preview resilience (#532)","tree":{"sha":"b3d8e04f11e07f79fcfec5a15414a7bbd146556e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/b3d8e04f11e07f79fcfec5a15414a7bbd146556e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKDg0NzY3ZWU1MTMzZmYzNmJjZjVjODY1ZTk3ZDM3MzcwNjlhOWFlMTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636","sha":"8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636"}],"sha":"84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/84767ee5133ff36bcf5c865e97d3737069a9ae11"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-17T17:28:01Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-17T17:28:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Reset kro helm values on every operator upgrade (#529)\n\nhelm upgrade with no provided values silently reuses the previous\nrelease''s values. That is how the retired fork''s image pin\n(kro-multicluster:v0.0.1-mc.7) survived the chart switch to upstream\nkro and kept the fork binary running in faros-dev after #528 deployed.\n--reset-values makes every upgrade converge on chart defaults + the\nCR''s explicit overrides, regardless of what the last release carried.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"02c0414b1a6d305398366ae2d01d4757b321535d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/02c0414b1a6d305398366ae2d01d4757b321535d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKDhjNmE2ZGQ0NDE0NWNiMjIxOWExYmRiYzhiZDZkOTBhZTk3ZGQ2MzY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e","sha":"efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e"}],"sha":"8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/8c6a6dd44145cb2219a1bdbc8bd6d90ae97dd636"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-17T09:56:13Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-17T09:56:13Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Flatten the infrastructure API to one Instance kind and run upstream kro (#528)\n\nEvery product used to be its own GVR (applications, searxngs, browsers, …),\nminted per Template as a dynamic CRD + APIResourceSchema + APIExport entry.\nAdding a product meant new permission claims, re-accepted bind grants, and\nhand-synced plural lists across every consumer. The tenant surface is now\npermanently two resources — templates (unchanged, virtual) and instances —\nwith the product selected by spec.template and the template-shaped input in\nspec.values, so a new Template is one YAML file and nothing else moves.\n\nProvider:\n- New Instance kind (cluster-scoped; values preserve-unknown; status =\n  typed baseline + mirrored backend fields), installed at init like\n  templates. Per-template CRD/schema synthesis deleted.\n- New instancespec package recreates what the per-template CRDs did at\n  admission (reserved farosMode/farosActions* injection, structural\n  defaulting, openapi + CEL validation); invalid instances report\n  Valid=False instead of being rejected.\n- New controller/instance (replaces controller/application): validates\n  values, runs the exposure gate (per-kind flags now derived from the\n  Template instead of a hardcoded GVK table), stamps\n  fqdn/farosCluster/credentialsSecretName into values, bridges the OIDC and\n  registry-pull Secrets, materializes the per-template kro CR in the\n  tenant''s runtime namespace, and mirrors its status back (runtimeRef makes\n  deletion survive Template retirement).\n- Data plane serves only the instances resource and resolves the contract\n  via the fetched instance''s spec.template; MCP server and portal drop all\n  kind discovery (the portal loses its introspection index entirely).\n- Access-gate SARs target instances/<name> subresource access.\n\nkro:\n- RGD instance kinds are Namespaced on the runtime cluster; buildRGD strips\n  the authored placeholder namespaces and rewrites RBAC ServiceAccount\n  subjects to ${schema.metadata.namespace} (the job the fork''s\n  deploy-to-local-runtime localization used to do).\n- The faroshq/kro-multicluster fork is retired: kro never watches kcp\n  anymore (the instance controller bridges kcp -> runtime), so we deploy\n  upstream oci://registry.k8s.io/kro/charts/kro (>= 0.9.3, which contains\n  both fixes the fork carried). No kcp kubeconfig is seeded onto the\n  runtime cluster; multicluster/deployToLocalRuntime wiring removed from\n  the operator, Makefile, and Tiltfiles. Chart CRDs are applied explicitly\n  on every reconcile because helm never upgrades crds/-dir CRDs.\n- applyRGD now skips no-op updates - the unconditional update bumped the\n  RGD generation every pass and made kro re-reconcile every instance.\n\nConsumers (breaking, no compat): vibe-studio, app-studio, and agents claim\nexactly instances; ResourceRefs pin Instance/instances; desired instance\nspecs wrap as {template, values}; dataplane refs use the instances segment.\nExisting tenant instances and runtime clusters with the old cluster-scoped\nCRDs must be recreated; the fork->upstream helm upgrade rebuilds all\nruntime workloads (kcp state re-materializes them automatically).\n\nDocs: docs/infrastructure-flattened-instances.md describes the new design;\nthe old architecture doc points at it.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d1ed38c5f89bf366e5d4e0fb51bbb30fd110aa88","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/d1ed38c5f89bf366e5d4e0fb51bbb30fd110aa88"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e","node_id":"C_kwDOS3qZj9oAKGVmYTRjOThjMzIzMTA1YTc0M2FiMjE3ZTNjN2E3Yjg3NmJkNGNiMmU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commit/a764ae2db2628cde0debab2c3d970425ec041c39","sha":"a764ae2db2628cde0debab2c3d970425ec041c39","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/a764ae2db2628cde0debab2c3d970425ec041c39"}],"sha":"efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/efa4c98c323105a743ab217e3c7a7b876bd4cb2e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3225744?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/mjudeikis","id":3225744,"login":"mjudeikis","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyMjU3NDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mjudeikis","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/commits/a764ae2db2628cde0debab2c3d970425ec041c39/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-17T09:56:04Z","email":"mangirdas@judeikis.lt","name":"Mangirdas Judeikis"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-17T09:56:04Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Restart app sign-in on stale state instead of dead-ending (#523)\n\nLeaving an app tab open past the 5 minute stateTTL stranded the user on\nan opaque \"invalid app access state\" 400, with no way forward but to\nfind the app''s URL again by hand. The round trip through the hub, Dex\nand GitHub exceeds that TTL easily on a slow environment, so this was\nroutine rather than exceptional.\n\nconsumeReturnState already distinguished five causes internally and then\ncollapsed all of them into that one status. Return them instead, and\nrestart the sign-in for the ones a fresh round trip actually fixes: an\nexpired state and an undecodable cookie. The original deep link is\ncarried across the restart, so the user lands where they asked to be.\n\nThe restart is only offered when a cookie came back. That is the whole\nloop guard: a returned cookie is the evidence this browser round-trips\nours, so a new nonce will come back too. With no cookie at all — blocked,\nor a callback URL copied into a different browser — a restart would mint\nanother cookie that is dropped just the same, and the app would bounce\nbetween the gate and the hub for as long as the user waited. That case\nkeeps terminating with an error. This is the loop already documented in\npkg/hub/appauth, which was observed minting ~8 sessions/second. A retry\nmarker would not help here regardless: the hub replaces RawQuery when it\nredirects to redirect_uri, so nothing survives the round trip except the\ncookie.\n\nTwo causes deliberately keep their 400. A state that does not match the\ncookie may belong to a different sign-in the browser still has in\nflight, and restarting would overwrite that cookie — breaking a working\nlogin to fix a callback that is most likely forged or hand-copied. A\nreplayed state is an attack signal, and the benign refresh-after-login\ncase never reaches it because success already cleared the cookie.\n\nLog lines now separate \"recovering\" from \"rejected\" so the two are\ndistinguishable in the gate.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"5d80b20dfb1be58f1acd58a962557450f2f07172","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/trees/5d80b20dfb1be58f1acd58a962557450f2f07172"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/railgrid/provider-infrastructure/git/commits/a764ae2db2628cde0debab2c3d970425ec041c39","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flo